    "updated_at": "2022-07-29T02:33:52Z",
    "body": "Yeah, good catch... the way that link element processing is specified is generally too vague and spread out. #7890 and #6450 are related, and #3000 sounds kind of like this. But the specific problem you point out is not tracked, so let's track this separately.\r\n\r\nIn particular, most rel types define \"appropriate times\" to fetch and process them, but there is no central handling that says when you start consulting these bullets.\r\n\r\nBrainstorming, I think something like the following would be appropriately centralized and rigorous while still allowing each link rel type to define its behavior appropriately:\r\n\r\n- Each link rel type defines \"process a link header\" and \"process a link element\" steps. There is no concept of defaulting, although there are helper algorithms they can call.\r\n\r\n- There is a central set of becomes-connected/becomes-disconnected/attribute-changed steps for `link` elements, which parses out the relevant attributes, including rel=\"\", type=\"\", and media=\"\". This then dispatches to \"process a link element\" for each parsed rel as appropriate, taking into account what you're noting here about only doing it on change, as well as perhaps taking into account which attributes each rel cares about. (E.g., preconnect cares about crossorigin=\"\", whereas preload=\"\" cares about as=\"\", type=\"\", and media=\"\"...)",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
